nce towards  russia and also during the course of the  week uh joe biden us president held a  video call with vladimir putin his  russian counterpart also to try and  spell out uh the western position now  short of actual armed conflict what  diplomatic tools does the west have in  its arsenal to counter russia yeah so we  do have to stress that at the beginning  ukraine's not say a nato member state  where the where the west would be uh uh  bound by uh treaty obligations to  intervene militarily but in that call  for example between joe biden and  vladimir putin biden essentially used  three sticks and one carrot  so the sticks were as uh his national  security advisor spoke afterwards first  of all to respond with very strong  economic sanctions now there are  long-standing sanctions against russia  anyway from from past actions against  crime uh for instance uh the annexation  of crimean but part of ukraine um but  there are escalations that the west  could apply for instance really going  for uh sanctions within the financial  system or something that would be a kind  of a sanctions nuclear option which  would be uh blocking russian access to  the swift payment system this is the  kind of thing that's been used for  example about against north korea in the  past that would have very very huge uh  implications for the russian economy  then the other sticks were providing  additional defense material to the  ukrainian so not actually sending in  troops but providing them with more  weapons potentially and then fortifying  uh the nato countries in the region  and one carrot offering to the russians  to have talks about their kind of bigger  overall concerns strategically in the  region the sense that russia often talks  about about feeling that the west nato  for example is constantly trying to  encroach towards their borders so biden  offered the carrot of talks on that okay  now this is of course the first g7  meeting for germany's new foreign  minister and elena berbach of the  environmentalist green party and she and  germany are going to be facing a lot of  pressure from other g7 leaders to  abandon the nord stream ii gas pipeline  over fears that that's going to increase  reliance on russian gas is there a  scenario under which you see this  actually happening yeah well it's  interesting isn't it so nordstrom 2 if  it was up to analina berbock and her  green party  that pipeline wouldn't be going ahead  anyway they've opposed it for a long  time but this is a coalition government  that's come in here in germany and the  social democrats were in charge the  chancellor's olaf schultz they've  broadly been more supportive but still a  russian invasion of ukraine would make  it really kind of stretch that  commitment to nordstrom 2 to breaking  limit and also earlier in the year the  us and germany  agreed a deal between the two sides that  meant that the us agreed to not apply  sanctions to german firms in incl  involved in nordstrom 2 under which the  germans  committed to this if russia attempted to  use energy as a weapon or committed  further aggressive acts against ukraine  germany will take action to limit  russian export capabilities to europe in  the energy sector including gas doesn't  name ns2 specifically nordstrom 2  but you know that's as close as you  could get to actually a commitment to  take some action like this richard  walker dw's chief international editor  thanks so much for joining me with that  update in the studio  and meanwhile germany's new chancellor  olaf schwarz has had his first call with  us president joe biden since taking  office they discussed those tensions on  the russia-ukraine border earlier  schultz was in brussels for talks with  european union and nato leaders where  again russia's troop build-up was high  on the agenda schultz said he wants to  revive negotiations with russia to  de-escalate tensions and avoid a  military conflict  olaf schultz began his day in paris with  a business lunch with french president  emmanuel macron  a quick bonjour in front of the elysee  palace then off to discuss franco-german  relations and challenges facing the eu  the new german chancellor said he was  optimistic about future cooperation  between europe's two biggest economies  from my point of view  there are now also good starting points  for what needs to be done next  they include germany's g7 presidency as  well as france's eu council presidency  the challenge is to ensure that we can  make great progress in all these areas  i'm sure that we will succeed  and this was a first visit on which we  can strengthen our cooperation and ties  a pressing issue on the agenda growing  tensions at the russian ukrainian border  the two leaders showed a united front  our ultimate goal is to prevent all  unnecessary tension  the common will of europeans and  americans is to show that we are very  vigilant and that there is no further  escalation  next stop for schultz the heart of the  eu itself  in brussels he met european commission  president ozula van der leyen and used  the chance to stress the role of germany  in the eu  germany is a european nation  we are a large country at the heart of  the european union  we have a large population a large  economy  and that is why german politics cannot  stand at the side and simply comment on  what's going on  relations with russia eu cooperation on  the pandemic response and the joint  effort to tackle climate change were  some of the topics discussed at the  commission's headquarters  later schultz also met with european  council president sean michel  michelle sets the agenda of the council  of eu leaders which is due to convene  next week  it will be the first such gathering for  schultz as german chancellor  he ended his marathon day with a last  meeting with nato secretary general jens  stoltenberg  again russia's troop build up on the  ukrainian border took center stage  schultz made clear a russian incursion  into ukrainian territory would be  unacceptable  dw's brussels bureau chief alexander von  naman has been following the chancellor  during his first trip to brussels and  she sent us this assessment  chancellor olaf schultz used his  inaugural trip to paris and brussels to  emphasize that his government stands for  continuity concerning germany's foreign  policy he also made clear that he is  eager to work closely together with  partners and eu institutions to  strengthen the european union and its  role on the international stage and that  message went down well in paris and in  brussels schultz is well known in  european capitals as merkel's former  vice chancellor and finance minister and  he knows exactly how the european union  works which gives him a flying start  here in brussels  however the honeymoon will soon be over  and schultz will be facing a number of  international challenges including  tensions with russia and fears that  moscow could be planning another  incursion into ukraine  the german chancellor warned russia that  massing troops on ukraine's border was  unacceptable and that any aggression  would have serious consequences  however he dodged questions whether in  that case germany would be willing to  hold the controversial north stream to  gas pipeline  there might be some tough choices ahead  for the new german chancellor  you
 

